<description>
Stretch the colored rectangular frame outward until its far edge reaches the lone marker pixel.

Input structure
- The background is the most common color.
- A single marker pixel is the only foreground color that appears once.
- All other foreground pixels form one solid rectangular frame with one border color and one interior color.

Rule
- If the marker is above or below the frame and its column lies within the frame, extend the frame vertically to the marker row.
- If the marker is left or right of the frame and its row lies within the frame, extend the frame horizontally to the marker column.
- Redraw the expanded rectangle with the same border and interior colors; everything outside remains background.

Key insight
The marker selects which side of the rectangle moves and where the new outer border lies; it is consumed by that border.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "transform_in_place",
  "src": "one two-color rectangular frame on the background plus a singleton marker pixel aligned with one side span",
  "transform": "erase the marker and redraw the frame expanded in the marker direction so the marker row or column becomes the new outer border; preserve the frame border and interior colors"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: 11-13 rows x 11-13 cols, output same size as input
  background: 0 (black)
  frame:
    shape: solid rectangle with 1-pixel border color and uniform interior color
    size: 3-5 rows x 3-5 cols
    colors: border and interior are two distinct non-bg non-marker colors
    placement: anywhere on grid with enough margin on at least one side for marker
  marker:
    color: 8 (azure) [observed]
    count: exactly 1 pixel
    alignment: orthogonally outside the frame; its row or col must lie within the frame span
    distance: 4-8 cells from nearest frame edge in the expansion direction
    constraint: marker col within [frame_c0, frame_c1] if above/below, or marker row within [frame_r0, frame_r1] if left/right
  transform:
    stretch the frame toward the marker so the marker row/col becomes the new outer border
    expanded frame keeps same border and interior colors; marker is consumed
    expanded frame must be >=3 in both dimensions and fit within grid bounds

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first
  steps:
    1. Pick grid size H x W in [11,13].
    2. Choose 3 distinct non-zero colors for border, interior, marker (marker=8 preferred).
    3. Pick expanded frame bbox (nr0,nc0,nr1,nc1) with both dims in [5,12], fitting in grid.
    4. Choose expansion direction (up/down/left/right) and shrink amount d in [4,8].
       Shrink the expanded frame on the chosen side by d to get original frame (must remain >=3 in both dims).
    5. Place marker pixel at distance d from the original frame edge, aligned within frame span.
       For up: marker at (nr0, random col in [c0,c1]). For right: marker at (random row in [r0,r1], nc1).
    6. Fill input grid: background, draw original frame (border+interior), place marker.
    7. Fill output grid: background, draw expanded frame (border+interior), no marker.
